findings.body:
In `generate_github_mirror_url_from_base`, the `set_path(github_url)` call was removed and the closure now just returns `cdn.to_string()`. The mirror base URL is returned unchanged, so every GitHub asset request under `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR` goes to the mirror root and never to the asset. WiX, NSIS, and AppImage tool downloads all fail or fail hash verification for anyone using this env var.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="33-40"}

::callout{type="warning"}
Nothing in the diff replaces the path-joining behavior, and no test covers the base-URL branch. The existing tests only exercise the template function, so this passes CI.

`download_webview2_offline_installer` was switched from `download(url)` to a direct `ureq::get(url)`. That call uses ureq's default agent, so it skips `base_ureq_agent()` entirely. The offline installer therefore does not get the platform verifier that the feature exists to provide, and it loses `Proxy::try_from_env`, which the old `download()` path honored. Behind a corporate proxy or with a private root CA, this download will fail even when the rest of the bundler works.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/windows/util.rs" lines="63-69"}

::callout{type="info"}
The bootstrapper download on line 49 still goes through `download()`, so the two WebView2 downloads now behave differently.

`generate_github_alternative_url` returns `ureq::agent()` for the mirror case, not `base_ureq_agent()`. Users who need the OS certificate store are most likely to be on a corporate network that also needs a mirror. For them, the mirror download still uses webpki roots and ignores the proxy.

::code{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="42-50"}

The proxy gap predates this PR, but the platform-certs part is new, and the PR's changelog says the bundler "will now read TLS Certificates installed on the system when downloading tools." That claim does not hold for mirror downloads.

The condition `if lock.is_some() && crate_lock_packages.is_empty()` was changed to `!crate_lock_packages.is_empty()`. In the enclosing else branch, `crate_lock_packages.len()` is never 1, so the old check only passed when there were zero matches. That made the join produce an empty string, which the following `is_empty` guard then discarded, so the block was effectively dead code. The fix makes `lock_version` populated whenever two or more lock entries match. That changes the CLI's crate-version output, for example `tauri (2.0.0, 2.1.0)`, in a PR that is supposed to be about TLS certificates. The changelog does not mention it.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/helpers/cargo_manifest.rs" lines="194-204"}

The `lock.is_some()` check is also redundant, since `crate_lock_packages` is already empty when there is no lock.

Both crates now default to `platform-certs`, so every user gets the OS verifier and the `rustls-platform-verifier` / `jni 0.19` dependency tree, not just those who opt in. This is the question the author raised in the PR description. My view: defaulting on changes behavior for everyone (a cert that the webpki store accepted may now be validated against the OS store, and vice versa), and it adds dependencies to every build. An opt-in feature or env var would be less disruptive, but a default-on flag is also defensible if the goal is to fix the recurring support reports.

::code{file="crates/tauri-bundler/Cargo.toml" lines="76-81"}

Whichever default is chosen, the changelog should say so explicitly, because it currently reads as an unconditional enhancement.
